# Academic Achievements

Selected academic publications, conference contributions and awards obtained during my studies in Aeronautical Engineering at **Politecnico di Milano**.

This folder contains supporting material for selected academic achievements related primarily to **experimental aerodynamics, aircraft design and aerospace engineering research**.

---

## Scientific Publication

### Wind Tunnel Investigation of Spoileron Effectiveness on a Low-Aspect-Ratio Swept Wing with Reflex Airfoils

**Authors:** Riccardo Andrew Oggioni, Carlo Emanuele Dionigi Riboldi, Filippo Coacci  
**Institution:** Politecnico di Milano  
**Conference:** 1st International Online Conference on Aerospace — IOCAE 2026  
**Publisher:** MDPI, Engineering Proceedings  
**Published:** August 2026

The work investigated the effectiveness of spoilerons as primary roll-control devices on a highly swept, low-aspect-ratio wing using experimental reflex airfoils.

The study combined preliminary analytical prediction with an extensive **wind-tunnel experimental campaign** to evaluate:

- spoileron spanwise position;
- chordwise position;
- spoileron geometry;
- deflection angle;
- rolling-moment effectiveness;
- adverse yaw behaviour;
- comparison with DATCOM predictions.

A set of normalised performance indices was introduced to separate the effects of spoiler area and moment arm from the intrinsic aerodynamic effectiveness of different configurations.

The experimental results were subsequently used to support control-surface sizing and configuration decisions for the aircraft under development.

### Publication

[`MDPI Conference Paper`](Wind_Tunnel_Investigation_Spoileron_Effectiveness.pdf)

---

## Best Poster Award — IOCAE 2026

The associated conference contribution received the **Best Poster Award** at the 1st International Online Conference on Aerospace.

The award recognised the quality of the research presentation and the underlying experimental work on spoileron effectiveness for unconventional swept-wing configurations.

The project formed part of a wider research activity involving:

**requirements → aerodynamic modelling → experimental testing → model validation → design decision**

---

## Experimental Contribution

My contribution to the research included work associated with:

- wind-tunnel experimental testing;
- test planning and execution;
- aerodynamic data processing;
- comparison between experimental and analytical predictions;
- control-surface effectiveness assessment;
- interpretation of roll and yaw behaviour;
- application of experimental findings to aircraft design.

The broader experimental activity was connected to my MSc thesis work on the development and validation of control solutions for an unconventional tail-sitter aircraft configuration.

---

## Key Technical Findings

The study highlighted several relevant aerodynamic behaviours:

- DATCOM predictions showed reasonable agreement with experimental **rolling-moment effectiveness**.
- Larger discrepancies were observed in the prediction of **yawing-moment coupling**.
- Spoiler effectiveness showed a clear dependence on spanwise and chordwise location.
- Increasing spoiler span was found to provide a more efficient improvement in control authority than an equivalent increase in chord for the configurations investigated.
- Experimental testing identified effective spoiler locations extending beyond the conventional DATCOM reference domain.

These results directly supported subsequent control-surface architecture and sizing decisions.

